From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 04:19:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: kaanapali-oneplus-iceland: camera rotation
 for landscape

The camera rotation values (rear 90, front 270) were copied from stock
Android's SENSOR_ORIENTATION. Android measures that angle against the
device's natural orientation, which on this tablet is portrait (`wm size`
2400x3392, ro.surface_flinger.primary_display_orientation =
ORIENTATION_270). Linux drives the 3392x2400 panel in its native landscape
orientation, and the DT rotation is relative to that. Re-expressed for the
landscape panel, both are 0: Android turns a front camera's angle the
opposite way from a rear camera's when the display rotates, so the
display offset that takes the front from 270 to 0 also takes the rear
from 90 to 0. A front frame captured with no rotation applied on the
tablet in landscape is upright, and the owner saw both viewfinders turned
the same way with 90 / 270.

With 90 / 270 the applications rotate the landscape frames by 90 degrees
and show a portrait viewfinder with the scene on its side.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
index 1355c4cfe97f..c6a36259d083 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
@@ -1309,7 +1309,8 @@ camera@10 {
 		avdd-supply = <&vreg_l14b_2p8>;
 
 		orientation = <0>;
-		rotation = <270>;
+		/* stock's SENSOR_ORIENTATION is relative to Android's portrait display; the landscape panel needs 0 */
+		rotation = <0>;
 
 		port {
 			cam_front_out: endpoint {
@@ -1349,7 +1350,8 @@ camera@36 {
 		avdd-supply = <&vreg_l14b_2p8>;
 
 		orientation = <1>;
-		rotation = <90>;
+		/* stock's SENSOR_ORIENTATION is relative to Android's portrait display; the landscape panel needs 0 */
+		rotation = <0>;
 
 		lens-focus = <&vcm>;
 
